`,s.jsxs(e.p,{children:[s.jsx(e.code,{children:"_flat"})," copies every parameter into a new, zero-padded vector. During the step, a rank therefore holds its parameters twice. The ",s.jsx(e.code,{children:".clone()"})," adds this rank's slice, another 1/N of them. ",s.jsx(e.code,{children:"grad_shard"})," holds 1/N of the gradients. ",s.jsx(e.code,{children:"clip_grad_norm_"})," creates it, and it stays alive until the step has applied it."]}),`
`,s.jsxs(e.p,{children:["The step runs after backward, when the activations are already freed. For the 0.58B model of ",s.jsx(e.code,{children:"configs/week-06.yaml"})," on four GPUs, ",s.jsx(e.code,{children:"flat"})," and the clone take ",h(D.staff.flat,1)," and ",h(D.staff.clone,1)," GB. Together that is about ",h(D.staff.total,1)," GB. That is less than the activations backward has just freed, so the peak does not rise. At 7B on four GPUs it does. The ",h(b.zero1.total,0),' GB in "Memory per rank" is the state of the ZeRO-1 design. On an 80 GB H100 it leaves ',h(b.zero1.headroom,0)," GB. Our code needs more. ",s.jsx(e.code,{children:"flat"})," alone takes ",h(D.flat,0)," GB, more than those ",h(b.zero1.headroom,0)," GB. With the clone (",h(D.clone,0)," GB) and ",s.jsx(e.code,{children:"grad_shard"})," (",h(D.gradShard,0)," GB), a rank holds at least ",h(D.stepPeak,0)," GB during the step. Even this undercounts. ",s.jsx(e.code,{children:"F.pad"})," inside ",s.jsx(e.code,{children:"_flat"})," always allocates new storage, so two full copies are alive for a moment. The list form of ",s.jsx(e.code,{children:"all_gather"})," allocates a full-size buffer of its own. The clip, just before, briefly makes its own ",h(D.gradients,0)," GB copy of the gradients, but frees it before the step starts. This implementation would not run a 7B model on four 80 GB GPUs."]}),`
`,s.jsxs(e.p,{children:["The copies keep the trainer and the model unchanged, and your code short. Without them, flat slices need every parameter to be a view into one shared buffer. Such views are easy to break. Our trainer's ",s.jsx(e.code,{children:"zero_grad(set_to_none=True)"})," throws the gradient views away at every step. A later ",s.jsx(e.code,{children:".to(device)"})," gives each parameter new storage. The clone is defensive: with it, input and output never overlap. It is not needed, though: an all-gather can read its input from the rank's own chunk of the output. On gloo the gold passes every test without it."]}),`
`,s.jsx(e.p,{children:"Production ZeRO keeps the parameters, and often the gradients, in persistent flat buffers. A simplified sketch:"}),`
